{"reply":"Your reading of the Caterpillar’s questioning as a test connects strongly with René Descartes’s *Meditation II*.

In Chapter 5, the Caterpillar opens with “Who are _you?_”; the exchange returns to that question instead of settling Alice’s identity. Descartes takes a related route: he strips away whatever can be doubted and arrives at “I am—I exist: this is certain,” treating thought as the firmest foothold left ([*Meditations on First Philosophy*, Meditation II](https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Meditations_on_First_Philosophy/Meditation_II)). The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy describes this as a method of using doubt to distinguish certainty from weaker conviction ([“Descartes’ Epistemology”](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/descartes-epistemology/)).

The parallel has a useful limit: both exchanges unsettle easy claims about the self, but Descartes uses questioning to seek an indubitable foothold, while the Caterpillar leaves Alice circling the question without supplying one. So the connection works best as a formal resemblance—not as a claim that Carroll is staging a literal Cartesian argument. Does Descartes’ promise that questioning can reach certainty sharpen the Caterpillar scene for you, or does it make Alice’s unresolved questioning feel more unsettling?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016","source_location":"Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar, source lines 960-1016","supported_claims":["In Chapter 5, the Caterpillar opens with “Who are _you?_”; the exchange returns to that question instead of settling Alice’s identity.","The parallel has a useful limit: both exchanges unsettle easy claims about the self, but Descartes uses questioning to seek an indubitable foothold, while the Caterpillar leaves Alice circling the question without supplying one.","So the connection works best as a formal resemblance—not as a claim that Carroll is staging a literal Cartesian argument.","Does Descartes’ promise that questioning can reach certainty sharpen the Caterpillar scene for you, or does it make Alice’s unresolved questioning feel more unsettling?"],"exact_quote":"“Who are _you?_”"},{"source_kind":"web","evidence_id":"https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Meditations_on_First_Philosophy/Meditation_II","supported_claims":["Descartes takes a related route: he strips away whatever can be doubted and arrives at “I am—I exist: this is certain,” treating thought as the firmest foothold left.","The parallel has a useful limit: both exchanges unsettle easy claims about the self, but Descartes uses questioning to seek an indubitable foothold, while the Caterpillar leaves Alice circling the question without supplying one.","So the connection works best as a formal resemblanc